{  
• Psittacosis (also known as parrot fever) is relatively rare in humans and is typically treatable with antibiotics, which can reduce the urgency for developing a dedicated human vaccine.  
• Historical efforts to produce a fully licensed human vaccine have not gained significant traction, likely due to limited large-scale demand.  
• As of recent information, there is no indication of any major, near-term clinical trials toward a human Psittacosis vaccine.  
}  

2. Provide reasons why the answer might be yes.  
{  
• Outbreaks in bird populations, especially among pet birds like parrots, may stimulate research into a preventive measure, possibly for both birds and humans.  
• Advances in vaccine technology could make it more feasible to rapidly develop vaccines for rare or emerging diseases.  
• Heightened attention to zoonotic diseases (diseases that jump from animals to humans) might lead to new research funding and projects.  
}  

3. Aggregate your considerations.  
{  
Considering the current state of research and the relatively low prioritization for a Psittacosis vaccine—contrasted with the possibility that new vaccine technologies could accelerate development—it seems more likely that a publicly recognized and widely available human vaccine will not be in place by late 2024. While there may be some efforts toward avian vaccines or experimental human vaccines, it does not seem probable that an official, widely adopted vaccine for Psittacosis will be confirmed on Wikipedia by the target date.  
}  

